               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Introduction\n\nIn the previous class, we learned that division and multiplication are related. We also visualized this relationship with squares, showing how the product of a multiplication can be shared among values. However, we have not yet seen how to carry out this sharing in practice.\n\nIn this class, we will learn a method for doing division, and we will see how it can also show whether our results are correct.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "3eba8b4b-bd9d-4c04-9025-63815b94e87f",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "3eba8b4b-bd9d-4c04-9025-63815b94e87f",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "happy",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "4742a2d6-a3c5-4883-beb5-8ed0240fa9a7",
               "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
               "page": "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/LongDivision.html",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            },
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "8d6586b8-178b-4806-a050-ef5a8704f5e1",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=8d6586b8-178b-4806-a050-ef5a8704f5e1",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Dividing a Product\n\nThe **product** is the name given to the result of a multiplication. Our goal in this class is to divide the product into different groups. These groups can receive equal values, or they can leave a remaining group.\n\nLet us look at an example of a product:\n\n**12 = 4 × 3**",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p data-custom=\"division-recipe-groups\"></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "965ccef0-5e99-4408-854e-4e248338403f",
         "prev": "9ec2c47d-4325-4ead-b4c6-4f8c3b04c8d9",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "965ccef0-5e99-4408-854e-4e248338403f",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "happy",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "4742a2d6-a3c5-4883-beb5-8ed0240fa9a7",
               "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
               "page": "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/LongDivision.html",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            },
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "8d6586b8-178b-4806-a050-ef5a8704f5e1",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=8d6586b8-178b-4806-a050-ef5a8704f5e1",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Reorganizing by 4\n\nDivision can also be written in different ways. Use the buttons below to see the two representations.\n\nIn this division, we are representing 12 divided by 4, which gives 3 with a remainder of 0.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p data-custom=\"division-recipe-12-4\"></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "a186680c-5234-404e-9364-587a8109a4f8",
         "prev": "3eba8b4b-bd9d-4c04-9025-63815b94e87f",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "a186680c-5234-404e-9364-587a8109a4f8",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "thinking",
         "source_menu": [],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Thinking\n\nWe have written the multiplication and the division in different formats. Is it possible to verify whether the result of the division is correct?",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "89951032-8b3f-47a0-b795-57738a6d11e4",
         "prev": "965ccef0-5e99-4408-854e-4e248338403f",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": "yes",
         "score": 2,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "89951032-8b3f-47a0-b795-57738a6d11e4",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "inlove",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "4742a2d6-a3c5-4883-beb5-8ed0240fa9a7",
               "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
               "page": "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/LongDivision.html",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            },
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "8d6586b8-178b-4806-a050-ef5a8704f5e1",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=8d6586b8-178b-4806-a050-ef5a8704f5e1",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### The Connection\n\nThe parts of the long division method we are learning have names. We can connect the elements of the division with the relationship of multiplication, as shown below:\n\n**Dividend = Quotient × Divisor + Remainder**\n\n**12 = 3 × 4 + 0**\n\nWhen we multiply the quotient by the divisor and add the remainder, we recover the dividend. This is a way to check whether the division is correct.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "6e0aa2f0-4258-4ccf-9754-5d437342a954",
         "prev": "a186680c-5234-404e-9364-587a8109a4f8",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "6e0aa2f0-4258-4ccf-9754-5d437342a954",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "shocking",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "4742a2d6-a3c5-4883-beb5-8ed0240fa9a7",
               "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
               "page": "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/LongDivision.html",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            },
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "8d6586b8-178b-4806-a050-ef5a8704f5e1",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=8d6586b8-178b-4806-a050-ef5a8704f5e1",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### The Hidden Element\n\nWhen we multiply two numbers, we find all the same elements we observed in the division. The only exception is the addition of the remainder, which is not there. The reason is that the remainder is always zero, and, as we have already learned, adding zero to any number does not change the result:\n\n**12 = 3 × 4** (multiplication)\n\n**12 = 3 × 4 + 0** (checking the division result)",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "7939473d-5964-4d0e-99ea-819aeaae30de",
         "prev": "89951032-8b3f-47a0-b795-57738a6d11e4",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "7939473d-5964-4d0e-99ea-819aeaae30de",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "nerd",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "4742a2d6-a3c5-4883-beb5-8ed0240fa9a7",
               "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
               "page": "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/LongDivision.html",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            },
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "8d6586b8-178b-4806-a050-ef5a8704f5e1",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=8d6586b8-178b-4806-a050-ef5a8704f5e1",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### A Slight Difference\n\nTo reinforce what we have learned, instead of dividing 12 by 4, let us divide **13 by 4**:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p data-custom=\"division-recipe-13-4\"></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "As you can see, the remainder is now different from zero. This shows that 13 is not divisible by 4:\n\n**13 = 3 × 4 + 1** (checking the division result)",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "d46ba3d4-4a87-48d8-bdc7-5abe44d675b1",
         "prev": "6e0aa2f0-4258-4ccf-9754-5d437342a954",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "d46ba3d4-4a87-48d8-bdc7-5abe44d675b1",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "party",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "4742a2d6-a3c5-4883-beb5-8ed0240fa9a7",
               "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
               "page": "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/LongDivision.html",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            },
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "8d6586b8-178b-4806-a050-ef5a8704f5e1",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=8d6586b8-178b-4806-a050-ef5a8704f5e1",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Conclusion\n\nIn this class, we learned the long division method, also called the long division algorithm. It allows us to express a division in several steps and provides a proper way to verify whether the result is correct.\n\nWhenever you divide, remember: **Dividend = Quotient × Divisor + Remainder**.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
